---
import CaseStudy from '../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro';
import Claim from '../../components/Claim.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import FaultStrip from '../../components/FaultStrip.astro';
import Pipeline from '../../components/Pipeline.astro';
import type { ClaimId } from '../../data/claims';

const sources: ClaimId[] = [
  'ae.verifier.checks', 'ae.fi.classes', 'ae.fi.faults', 'ae.fi.falseBlocks', 'ae.unmetPenalty', 'ae.tests.now',
];
const toc = [
  { id: 'what', label: 'What it is' },
  { id: 'why', label: 'Why the model stopped allocating' },
  { id: 'pipeline', label: 'Who decides at each step' },
  { id: 'objective', label: 'The objective' },
  { id: 'verifier', label: 'Checking the checker' },
  { id: 'audit', label: 'The audit chain' },
  { id: 'not-done', label: 'What is not done' },
  { id: 'reproduce', label: 'Reproduce' },
];
---
<CaseStudy
  title="AegisOps"
  dek="Flood reports in, solver-backed response plans out. A person approves each one, every number is re-checked, and every decision can be replayed."
  description="A research platform for crisis-response planning: a language model reads field reports, a CP-SAT solver allocates units on road travel times, pure-function checks gate every plan, and a person approves."
  year="2026"
  stack={['Python', 'OR-Tools CP-SAT', 'OSRM', 'OpenStreetMap', 'FastAPI', 'PostGIS', 'React', 'Playwright', 'OpenTelemetry']}
  links={[
    { label: 'Code', href: 'https://github.com/Avnish1505/aegisops-ai' },
    { label: 'Write-up', href: 'https://avnish1505.github.io/aegisops-ai-blog/' },
  ]}
  sources={sources}
  toc={toc}
  ogImage="/og/aegisops.png"
>
  <h2 id="what">What it is</h2>
  <p>
    AegisOps is a research platform for planning a response to a flood. It runs a fictional exercise set on real Lucknow places:
    hospitals, police and fire stations and roads from OpenStreetMap. Field reports come in English, Hindi or Hinglish. The system
    proposes which units go where, shows its working, and waits for a person.
  </p>
  <p>
    It never dispatches anything. Every plan needs human approval, a blocked plan cannot be approved, whoever proposed a plan cannot
    approve it, and model output reaches an operator only after deterministic checks. It has no connection to any emergency service.
  </p>

  <h2 id="why">Why the model stopped allocating</h2>
  <p>
    The first version asked a language model for the whole plan: which unit goes where, and how long it takes to get there.
    Nothing guaranteed that the units existed, were free, or were used once, that the travel times were real, or that the plan
    was any good. The safety gate I wrote around it took the model's own status at face value, so a wrong plan could still look
    ready to approve.
  </p>
  <p>
    Allocation is a small, well-posed optimisation problem, and exact solvers are good at those. So a CP-SAT solver now makes every
    plan, and the model does the two jobs a solver cannot: reading messy reports and drafting prose. The old model engine is kept as
    an experiment arm, and its output goes through the same checks as the solver's.
  </p>

  <h2 id="pipeline">Who decides at each step</h2>
  <p>Pick a step. The colour says who is in charge of it.</p>
  <Pipeline />

  <h2 id="objective">The objective</h2>
  <p>
    The solver and the verifier share one written-down definition of a good plan, so a reviewer can argue with it:
  </p>
  <math display="block" aria-label="Minimise the sum over incidents i of weight w i times P times unmet demand u i, plus the sum over assigned pairs of unit r and incident i of w i times the road travel time t r i">
    <mrow>
      <mo movablelimits="false">min</mo>
      <mspace width="0.4em"></mspace>
      <munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>·</mo><mi class="t-pen">P</mi><mo>·</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub>
      <mo>+</mo>
      <munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></munder><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>·</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub>
    </mrow>
  </math>
  <p>
    Here <em>w<sub>i</sub></em> is the severity weight of incident <em>i</em>, times any priority an operator adds,
    <em>u<sub>i</sub></em> is the number of units it needed and did not get, and <em>t<sub>ri</sub></em> is the road travel time from
    unit <em>r</em>, taken from the OSRM matrix and never from the plan's own claim. The penalty <em>P</em> is <Claim id="ae.unmetPenalty" /> minutes,
    so covering a requirement is always worth more than any realistic drive and the solver never leaves demand unmet to save time.
    Hard rules sit outside the objective: a unit goes to at most one incident, only if it is available and of a type that incident needs, and no incident gets more of a type than it asked for.
  </p>

  <h2 id="verifier">Checking the checker</h2>
  <p>
    Every plan, from the solver or the model, goes through <Claim id="ae.verifier.checks" /> checks written as pure functions. To test
    them, I injected one fault at a time into 50 seeded scenarios, one fault class per run, and asked whether the right check failed.
  </p>
  <FaultStrip />
  <FaultStrip table />
  <p>
    Two classes are deliberately non-blocking. A plan worse than the optimum is shown to the operator as a high-severity finding, and
    instruction-like text in a report is flagged, never obeyed.
  </p>

  <h2 id="audit">The audit chain</h2>
  <p>An insert-only table proves nothing if someone can edit rows directly. So every step appends an event to one chain:</p>
  <math display="block" aria-label="h n equals SHA-256 of h n minus 1 concatenated with the canonical JSON of the event's id, time, actor, type and payload">
    <mrow>
      <msub><mi>h</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo>
      <mtext>SHA-256</mtext><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>‖</mo>
      <mtext>json</mtext><mo>(</mo><mtext>id, time, actor, type, payload</mtext><mo>)</mo><mo>)</mo>
    </mrow>
  </math>
  <p>
    Editing any event, or a decision or approval row it hashes, breaks the chain, and an endpoint walks it and reports the first broken link.
    One gap is stated plainly in the console too: deleting the newest event cannot be detected from inside the database. That needs the
    head hash stored somewhere else.
  </p>

  <h2 id="not-done">What is not done</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>No live-model results.</strong> The report reader, the constraint translator, drafting and model allocation have only run against mocked or oracle models. The evaluation harness is built. No accuracy, latency or cost is claimed.</li>
    <li><strong>Model against solver is not measured.</strong> The experiment exists and has not been run, for the same reason.</li>
    <li><strong>Road times are free-flow.</strong> OSRM's car profile knows nothing about traffic, closures or flooding.</li>
    <li><strong>Fault injection is not an attack.</strong> The faults come from mutators written alongside the verifier, and the scenarios use straight-line travel times, not the road network.</li>
    <li><strong>The exercise is fictional,</strong> unit counts at facilities are an assumption, and OpenStreetMap's facility coverage is incomplete.</li>
    <li>There is no real sign-in and the public demo is configured but not deployed.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    An earlier report showed the model engine "blocked" on every scenario. It was produced without a model key, so it measured the
    fallback path. The repository marks it superseded, and so does this page.
  </p>

  <h2 id="reproduce">Reproduce</h2>
  <p>At the linked commit, <Claim id="ae.tests.now" /> tests pass. Lint, types, a PostGIS job, the console's tests and a Playwright run with axe accessibility checks also run in CI.</p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" label="terminal" code={`git clone https://github.com/Avnish1505/aegisops-ai
cd aegisops-ai
pip install -r requirements-dev.txt
pytest
python scripts/fault_report.py --check   # fails if the committed fault report is out of date`} />
</CaseStudy>

